Z||000014||Adrift||11-24-2001||11:01 AM||||And Spyder, while we're on the kitcar topic, what type of bracket did you use to keep the front clip area right in front of the door secured? On my kit they are about 4" away from the side of the car, and aren't hooked up to anything... And any info on brackets to hold the rear clip out would be appreciated as well||||reg||1
Z||000015||Spyder||11-24-2001||11:11 AM||||Rogergarrison<BR>You didn’t bother me, everyone has their own view on cars, if everyone liked the same thing it would really suck.<BR>All it needs is the windshield, exhaust rerouted, and interior put back together, and a few finishing touches.<P> Adrift<BR>You should be able to find them locally. They are imported from Australia. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Daleford||11-24-2001||11:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spyder:<BR><B>Those wheels are ROH ZS: <A HREF="http://www.jdwheels.com/roh_zs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jdwheels.com/roh_zs.htm</A> <BR>17x7.5 $200 each<BR>17x9.5 $238 each<BR>For a total of $876.00<BR>Best price for the look and size.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Nice look Spyder.  I assume you had to use wheel adapters to bolt the wheels on? Or are these custom wheels made for a rebody project.<BR>What bolt patterns are you using, stock Fiero?  Thanking you in advance.<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spyder||11-24-2001||11:45 AM||||   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bracket2.JPG">   <BR>On the front you can use flat metal and bend it, once installed it can be bonded in to be more rigid.<BR>On the rear you can use square pipe behind the rear wheel at the bottom, then in front of the rear wheel you can use flat or square stock. Weld a small rectangle piece to the end of the bracket that goes against the fiberglass to help distribute the load and bond it in.  This will keep from having any stress cracks. You can see the front rear  bracket through the rear air duct,which is not finished.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.markalexandermiller.com/m2.jpg">  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Spyder||11-24-2001||11:52 AM||||Wheel adapter/spacers were used, the bolt pattern is 5x4.5 and went from 12mm studs to 1/2 inch studs to be stronger. <BR>By getting the largest offsets on the wheels the adapters were only 2 inches in the rear and 2.25 inches on the front.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Spyder||11-24-2001||11:54 AM||||Remeber:<BR>You can put brackets anywhere you need them.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Adrift||11-24-2001||02:28 PM||||Spyder, thanks, thats gives me a better idea - Just out of curiosity, how much have you spent on your Kit to get it to its current point? I haven't even got the body mounted properly and am over my original budget!||||reg||1
Z||000021||Daleford||11-24-2001||09:44 PM||||Spyder, where did you get those great looking rear view mirrors?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000022||Spyder||11-24-2001||10:07 PM||||I ordered the lights from Mercedes that go on the slk. Then I cut the mirrors and the lights to fit.  I would have probably been easier to have bought the housings also. <P><BR>I have spent too much money on this project….<BR>I tore the Fiero down to nothing and rebuilt it from the ground up with all performance, racing, and custom made parts. I will never go this extravagant ever again.<BR>(maybe if I won Loto)<P>||||reg||1
